"""Week44 Days 5-6: preparation and a complete offline learning project."""

L(4, '1. Handle missing measurements without inventing answers',
  'Fit a numeric imputation rule on training data and state its limitations.',
  [('SimpleImputer; statistics_', 'SimpleImputer learns a replacement such as each training column’s median. statistics_ stores that fitted replacement vector.'),
   ('Missingness; NaN', 'A missing measurement is not a measured zero. NumPy NaN represents a missing numeric value in these examples; missing targets are a separate labelling problem.')],
  'A missing petal width does not mean a zero-width petal. Replacing all unknown values with zero can introduce an artificial pattern and distort distances. A simple baseline preparation rule replaces missing numeric inputs with a median learned from the training partition. This creates a usable table, not a newly observed measurement.\n\nFit the imputer on training rows only. The held-out values must not change its replacement statistics. The final Iris data is complete, but including the explicit imputation step lets us demonstrate inference with a partially missing measurement. The checked prediction function rejects an entirely unmeasured flower because a plausible label there would be unsupported by any supplied measurement.\n\nInspect why data is absent. If missingness depends on collection practice or the target, a median may hide useful information or create bias. A completely empty training column also needs an explicit policy; do not assume every imputer can learn a meaningful median from no observed values. Missing labels cannot be solved by filling them with the model’s guesses and pretending they are verified truth.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer\ntrain = [[1.0, 10.0], [3.0, np.nan], [np.nan, 30.0]]\nimputer = SimpleImputer(strategy="median")\nfilled = imputer.fit_transform(train)\nprint("Training medians:", imputer.statistics_.tolist())\nprint("Filled train:", filled.tolist())\nprint("New row:", imputer.transform([[np.nan, 100.0]]).tolist())',
  'Use median imputation for [2,8,NaN] and print the learned replacement.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer\ni = SimpleImputer(strategy="median").fit([[2.0], [8.0], [np.nan]])\nprint("Replacement:", i.statistics_.tolist())\nprint("Missing new value:", i.transform([[np.nan]]).tolist())',
  'Explain in output why a missing target is not an observed training label.',
  'rows = [{"length": 5.0, "label": 0}, {"length": 6.0, "label": None}]\nlabelled = [row for row in rows if row["label"] is not None]\nprint("Verified labelled rows:", len(labelled))\nprint("Investigate missing labels; do not invent ground truth")',
  [('Where must the imputation median be learned?', 'Training inputs only', 'All rows before splitting', 'Final test answers', 'Replacement statistics are fitted state and need the training boundary.'),
   ('Does an imputed measurement become an observed fact?', 'No; it remains a replacement assumption', 'Always', 'Only when rounded', 'A preparation rule does not recover the unknown true measurement.')],
  'Treat every missing measurement as a genuine zero and every missing label as class0.', 'Use a documented training-only input policy and investigate missing target labels separately.',
  'The first median uses observed1 and3; the second uses10 and30. The future100 never participates in those medians. transform reuses the fitted replacements.')

L(4, '2. Scale for distance while preserving the training reference',
  'Explain why standardisation matters for nearest-neighbour similarity.',
  [('Euclidean distance', 'The square root of summed squared feature differences. Large numerical scales can dominate this default nearest-neighbour distance.'),
   ('ddof=0; constant columns', 'StandardScaler uses population variance, equivalent to NumPy std with ddof=0. A constant training feature receives scale1; it cannot supply variation by itself.')],
  'A distance model compares numbers, so feature units are part of the algorithm. If one measurement is stored in millimetres and another in centimetres, the former can have a much larger numerical range even when the physical variations are similar. StandardScaler learns a reference centre and spread for each training column.\n\nThe chart contrasts raw values with transformed training values for one fictional measurement. The right panel is expressed in training standard-deviation units, not centimetres. It does not mean the data became normally distributed or that outliers disappeared. On a constant column, standardisation cannot create information.\n\nWhether to scale depends on the method. Distance-based models commonly need attention to scale; some tree methods are much less affected by monotonic feature rescaling. Do not make blanket claims that every algorithm requires the same preprocessing. Here scaling belongs in the pipeline because the chosen classifier compares distances, and fitting it on training only preserves the evaluation boundary.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nraw = np.array([[10.0], [20.0], [30.0]])\ns = StandardScaler().fit(raw)\nz = s.transform(raw).ravel()\nprint("Centre:", s.mean_.tolist())\nprint("Scale:", s.scale_.round(3).tolist())\nprint("Standardised:", z.round(3).tolist())\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(7, 3.5))\naxes[0].bar(["A", "B", "C"], raw.ravel(), color="#376ea6")\naxes[0].set(title="Raw measurement", ylabel="Example units")\naxes[1].bar(["A", "B", "C"], z, color="#3d927a")\naxes[1].axhline(0, color="#555555", linewidth=0.8)\naxes[1].set(title="Training-standardised", ylabel="Training SD units")\nfig.tight_layout()\nfig.savefig("chart.png", dpi=110)\nplt.close(fig)',
  'Show that a constant feature produces zeros rather than division by zero.',
  'from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\ns = StandardScaler().fit([[5.0], [5.0], [5.0]])\nprint("Scale:", s.scale_.tolist())\nprint("Transformed:", s.transform([[5.0], [5.0]]).ravel().tolist())',
  'Calculate raw distance contributions when one feature difference is1 and the other100.',
  'import numpy as np\ndifference = np.array([1.0, 100.0])\nprint("Squared contributions:", (difference ** 2).tolist())\nprint("Distance:", round(float(np.sqrt((difference ** 2).sum())), 3))',
  [('Why pay attention to feature scale in KNN?', 'Large numeric ranges can dominate distances', 'Scaling adds verified labels', 'It makes every distribution Gaussian', 'Nearest neighbours are selected using numeric separation.'),
   ('Does scaling turn a constant feature into useful variation?', 'No', 'Yes', 'Only with an integer dtype', 'A transformation cannot invent observed variation.')],
  'Describe a standardised value as a centimetre measurement clipped to0–1.', 'Describe it relative to training mean and spread, retaining original units in the input contract.',
  'Both panels show the same three training observations. The right values are (raw -20)/population SD. Their changed units explain the new coordinate system used by a distance model.',
  figure=('Three fictional raw measurements beside their standardised values', 'The panels use different y-axis units. Standardisation changes representation, not the underlying observations; the numeric output gives the exact rounded values.'))

L(4, '3. Encode categories without inventing a ranking',
  'Fit a categorical vocabulary on training rows and handle unseen categories explicitly.',
  [('OneHotEncoder; categories_', 'OneHotEncoder learns observed training categories and creates an indicator column for each. categories_ records the learned vocabulary.'),
   ('handle_unknown; sparse_output', 'handle_unknown="ignore" represents an unseen category with zeros across its learned indicators. sparse_output=False returns a small dense array for these teaching tables; large problems may need sparse matrices.')],
  'A numeric estimator cannot generally treat arbitrary strings as numeric distances. Coding red=0, blue=1 and green=2 would introduce an ordering and distances that a colour name does not supply. One-hot encoding instead creates separate indicators without that invented rank.\n\nFit the vocabulary on training only. When a later row contains a colour not observed in training, the chosen ignore policy returns zeros for that feature’s indicators. It does not discover the new category’s meaning. Unknown handling should be recorded and monitored; a large number of unseen categories can signal a changed data source.\n\nThis fictional table demonstrates representation only. It is not a claim that a colour-only classifier is useful. Real categorical preparation also needs a policy for missing values and high-cardinality ids. An identifier with a different value on every row can be expensive to encode and can encourage memorisation rather than a reusable relationship.',
  'from sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder\nencoder = OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown="ignore", sparse_output=False)\ntrain = [["red"], ["blue"], ["red"]]\nencoder.fit(train)\nprint("Vocabulary:", encoder.categories_[0].tolist())\nprint("Column names:", encoder.get_feature_names_out(["colour"]).tolist())\nprint("Red:", encoder.transform([["red"]]).tolist())\nprint("Unseen green:", encoder.transform([["green"]]).tolist())',
  'Encode tea/coffee and show the output for an unknown drink.',
  'from sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder\ne = OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown="ignore", sparse_output=False).fit([["tea"], ["coffee"]])\nprint("Names:", e.get_feature_names_out(["drink"]).tolist())\nprint("Unknown:", e.transform([["water"]]).tolist())',
  'Confirm that transforming an unseen value does not expand the fitted vocabulary.',
  'from sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder\ne = OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown="ignore", sparse_output=False).fit([["A"], ["B"]])\ne.transform([["C"]])\nprint("Still trained categories:", e.categories_[0].tolist())',
  [('What does ignore do for an unseen category?', 'Uses all zeros for its learned indicators', 'Learns the new category during transform', 'Guarantees a correct prediction', 'Unknown handling preserves the fitted column vocabulary without understanding the new value.'),
   ('Why can arbitrary category integers harm a distance model?', 'They invent numerical order and distance', 'Integers cannot be stored in arrays', 'They always reduce accuracy to zero', 'A nominal category code is not a measured numerical separation.')],
  'Assign arbitrary category numbers and interpret their differences as meaningful distances.', 'Use an appropriate categorical representation fitted only on training rows.',
  'get_feature_names_out reveals which fitted indicator each position represents. The unseen value leaves the vocabulary unchanged, which is essential for the downstream model’s fixed feature shape.')

L(4, '4. Give numeric and categorical columns their own preparation',
  'Use ColumnTransformer to compose a clear named-column contract.',
  [('ColumnTransformer; remainder', 'ColumnTransformer applies named transformations to selected columns and concatenates the results. remainder="drop" excludes unspecified columns instead of silently using them.'),
   ('Named Pipeline steps', 'Pipeline accepts (name, estimator) pairs. Explicit names such as fill and scale make fitted components inspectable through named_steps.')],
  'Mixed tables often need different preparation for different columns. Numeric distance and mass can receive median imputation and scaling; a category can receive most-frequent imputation and one-hot encoding. ColumnTransformer applies each branch to its selected columns and concatenates their outputs in the specified branch order.\n\nWrite explicit column lists. Dropping unspecified columns makes accidental inclusion of a target, source id or newly added field less likely, although it does not prove the chosen columns are safe. The correct feature contract must still be reviewed. get_feature_names_out helps trace the transformed positions.\n\nThe fictional table has four rows and is deliberately too small for a meaningful predictive evaluation. Its purpose is to inspect preprocessing mechanics, including a missing numeric value. The numeric branch scales imputed training values, so its learned centre describes the filled training table. No validation or future row contributes to either branch’s fitted state.',
  'import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler, OneHotEncoder\ntrain = pd.DataFrame({"mass": [1.0, 2.0, np.nan, 4.0], "colour": ["red", "blue", "red", "blue"]})\nnumeric = Pipeline([("fill", SimpleImputer(strategy="median")), ("scale", StandardScaler())])\nprep = ColumnTransformer([("number", numeric, ["mass"]), ("category", OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown="ignore", sparse_output=False), ["colour"])], remainder="drop")\nvalues = prep.fit_transform(train)\nprint("Prepared shape:", values.shape)\nprint("Prepared names:", prep.get_feature_names_out().tolist())\nprint("All finite:", bool(np.isfinite(values).all()))',
  'Build a transformer that explicitly keeps a numeric input but drops an id and target column.',
  'import pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\ntrain = pd.DataFrame({"measurement": [1.0, 2.0, 3.0], "row_id": [10, 11, 12], "target": [0, 1, 1]})\np = ColumnTransformer([("numeric", StandardScaler(), ["measurement"])], remainder="drop")\nprint("Output names:", p.fit(train).get_feature_names_out().tolist())',
  'Fit a categorical missing-value branch and transform an unknown future category.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder\np = Pipeline([("fill", SimpleImputer(strategy="most_frequent")), ("encode", OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown="ignore", sparse_output=False))])\np.fit([["red"], ["red"], ["blue"], [np.nan]])\nprint("Missing:", p.transform([[np.nan]]).tolist())\nprint("Unknown:", p.transform([["green"]]).tolist())',
  [('What does remainder="drop" do?', 'Excludes columns not assigned to a branch', 'Drops every row with a missing value', 'Automatically identifies target leakage', 'It is an explicit column policy, not an automatic leakage detector.'),
   ('Where should branch preparation learn its state?', 'Training rows passed to fit', 'The complete dataset first', 'Each future row separately', 'Both numerical statistics and category vocabularies are fitted state.')],
  'Apply one scaler to a table of numeric measurements and arbitrary text categories.', 'Assign reviewed column lists to appropriate numeric and categorical branches.',
  'The four input rows remain four output rows, while one numeric and two indicator columns form the transformed representation. Output names expose that composition.')

L(4, '5. Fit the entire preprocessing-and-model pipeline',
  'Keep preparation and prediction together and inspect fitted step state.',
  [('Pipeline.fit; Pipeline.predict', 'fit learns each transformer on the supplied training data and passes its transformed output onward. predict reuses those fitted transforms before the final predictor.'),
   ('named_steps', 'A mapping for inspecting explicitly named pipeline components. Inspection does not mean you should refit individual steps on held-out data.')],
  'Put the preparation object and classifier into one pipeline. The outer fit receives raw training inputs and labels. It fits preparation on training, transforms those rows and fits the classifier. predict takes raw future inputs, applies the fitted preparation and then predicts. You no longer have to remember a separate scaling call before every prediction.\n\nFor the Iris project, all four inputs are numeric. The lab therefore uses a simpler sequential median-imputer, scaler and classifier pipeline; a mixed-type ColumnTransformer is unnecessary there. Prefer the simplest structure that expresses the actual input contract.\n\nA pipeline is a useful boundary, not magic. If you call fit on all data, it learns from all data. If a selected input contains the future answer, the pipeline still uses it. The example makes the training-only mean inspectable and compares validation performance without touching the final project test. Weeks 45–47 build on this structure for model comparison and tuning.',
  'from lab import make_splits, build_model\nd = make_splits()\nmodel = build_model()\nmodel.fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nprint("Steps:", list(model.named_steps))\nprint("Scaler training rows:", int(model.named_steps["scale"].n_samples_seen_))\nprint("Validation accuracy:", round(model.score(d["X_valid"], d["y_valid"]), 3))',
  'Verify that the scaler mean matches the actual training feature means in this complete dataset.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom lab import make_splits, build_model\nd = make_splits()\nm = build_model().fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nprint("Train-only means match:", bool(np.allclose(m.named_steps["scale"].mean_, d["X_train"].mean())))',
  'Transform raw validation inputs through preparation without invoking the final classifier.',
  'from lab import make_splits, build_model\nd = make_splits()\nm = build_model().fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nprepared = m[:-1].transform(d["X_valid"])\nprint("Prepared validation shape:", prepared.shape)\nprint("Raw validation shape:", d["X_valid"].shape)',
  [('Can a pipeline fix a feature containing tomorrow’s answer?', 'No; review feature availability', 'Always', 'Only when the scaler is first', 'The pipeline does not know the real prediction-time meaning of a column.'),
   ('What happens during predict on the fitted pipeline?', 'Transform with saved state, then predict', 'Fit every step on the new batch', 'Train a new classifier automatically', 'Prediction must retain the training representation.')],
  'Fit the outer pipeline on all rows and assume pipelines automatically prevent leakage.', 'Call fit only on the allowed training partition and review every feature.',
  'The90-row scaler count is direct evidence about this fit. The validation score is computed after transformation by the fitted pipeline; no validation refit occurs.')

L(4, '6. Verify future input and unchanged fitted state',
  'Check columns, units, missingness and transformation stability before trusting inference.',
  [('Input schema; training-serving consistency', 'Future inputs need the same meanings, units and reviewed columns as training. Matching an array shape alone cannot verify semantic consistency.'),
   ('predict_checked', 'The supplied lab wrapper accepts a named DataFrame, restores the reviewed column order, rejects invalid measurements and maps predictions to names. It does not validate species identity or hidden unit mistakes.')],
  'A model can accept four numbers in the wrong order and still return a plausible category. Named columns help preserve meaning, but callers must still supply centimetres and the intended measurements. The lab wrapper requires exactly the four named fields, reorders them explicitly, rejects empty/non-numeric/infinite/nonpositive inputs and rejects entirely unmeasured rows.\n\nA partially missing measurement is allowed under the documented median policy. That does not make it as informative as a fully measured case. Unknown units or unrealistic but positive values may pass a numeric validator; contracts and appropriate range monitoring remain necessary. Do not claim that a small wrapper guarantees all real-world validity.\n\nCheck that predicting extreme unseen inputs cannot change the scaler’s mean or imputer’s median. This catches accidental inference-time fitting. The example also shows harmless column reordering: the wrapper restores the correct names before prediction. Raw unlabeled arrays do not provide that same safeguard.',
  'from lab import make_splits, build_model, predict_checked, FEATURES\nd = make_splits()\nm = build_model().fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nrows = d["X_valid"].iloc[:2]\na = predict_checked(m, rows)\nb = predict_checked(m, rows[FEATURES[::-1]])\nprint("Column order repaired:", a.equals(b))\nprint("Caller row ids retained:", a.index.equals(rows.index))',
  'Predict a huge validation batch and verify that scaler mean_ remains unchanged.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom lab import make_splits, build_model\nd = make_splits()\nm = build_model().fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nbefore = m.named_steps["scale"].mean_.copy()\nm.predict(d["X_valid"] * 1000)\nprint("Unchanged fitted mean:", bool(np.array_equal(before, m.named_steps["scale"].mean_)))',
  'Pass a negative measurement and print the clear validation error.',
  'from lab import make_splits, build_model, predict_checked\nd = make_splits()\nm = build_model().fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nrow = d["X_valid"].iloc[:1].copy()\nrow.iloc[0, 0] = -2.0\ntry:\n    predict_checked(m, row)\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    print(str(error))',
  [('Can a matching shape prove units and column meanings are correct?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for four features', 'Numeric structure and semantic meaning are separate input requirements.'),
   ('Should predict update the fitted scaler from new rows?', 'No', 'Yes to improve the score', 'Only for a large batch', 'Inference reuses training state; updating it changes the model procedure.')],
  'Accept any four positive numbers as verified centimetre flower measurements.', 'Validate structure and document semantic units, missingness and remaining limits.',
  'The wrapper uses column names to restore order and preserves the caller’s index. The state check addresses a separate concern: transformation must not silently refit during inference.')

L(5, '1. Start the real project with a data and scope audit',
  'Reconstruct the task and verify the actual training partition before fitting.',
  [('Audit trail', 'A record connecting the task, source, row identities, preprocessing, model settings and reported predictions.'),
   ('Educational benchmark', 'A small standard dataset that helps you practise a workflow. Success on it alone is not evidence that a model is ready for broader use.')],
  'Open docs/python-week44 and read lab.py alongside these parts. The project predicts a recorded Iris class from four centimetre measurements. It uses the dataset packaged with scikit-learn, so no download, account or GPU is required after installation. Its data is unrelated to Study Hub notes or progress.\n\nReconfirm the frozen 90/30/30 stratified split. Inspect only the training data while developing the procedure. The project can impute partial missing inputs, but the packaged training records here are complete. Keeping that distinction visible prevents you from describing a simulated preparation drill as an observed defect in this dataset.\n\nYour notebook or written notes should state the row unit, features, class names, seed, baseline and scope. Keep row ids for tracing errors rather than as predictive inputs. Treat the final test output shown later in this lesson as already seen once you read it; reproduce the run for learning and obtain new evaluation evidence for any later real claim.',
  'from lab import load_data, make_splits, LABELS\nX, y = load_data()\nd = make_splits()\nprint("Dataset:", X.shape, "labels:", len(y))\nprint("Classes:", LABELS)\nprint("Partitions:", [len(d["X_" + p]) for p in ["train", "valid", "test"]])\nprint("Training missing:", int(d["X_train"].isna().sum().sum()))',
  'Verify that all four feature columns are measurements with cm in their names.',
  'from lab import FEATURES\nprint("Features:", FEATURES)\nprint("Unit recorded:", all("(cm)" in name for name in FEATURES))',
  'Write two supported and two unsupported claims for this project.',
  'claims = {"reproducible interface exercise": True, "held-out prediction counts": True, "all wild flowers recognised": False, "causal effect of petal length": False}\nfor claim, supported in claims.items():\n    print(supported, claim)',
  [('What is the intended use of this project?', 'An educational three-class measurement workflow', 'Universal field species identification', 'Diagnosis from flower photos', 'The task and dataset impose a narrow scope.'),
   ('Are the final test results new unseen evidence after you read them here?', 'No; reproducing the demo is learning, not a new independent evaluation', 'Always', 'Only if the seed changes', 'Once inspected, the same test cannot support a claim of untouched evaluation after further tuning.')],
  'Treat a successful benchmark run as a ready-to-deploy identification service.', 'Describe the measured task, actual partitions and limits of the benchmark.',
  'The audit connects the public source data to the exact project setup. Partition sizes and missingness are observed facts, while deployment suitability remains unestablished.')

L(5, '2. Freeze the model and fit only the training partition',
  'Explain the final pipeline’s order and verify its fitted reference state.',
  [('Frozen model configuration', 'The project chooses median imputation, standard scaling and k=5 before inspecting final test performance. This is a declared procedure, not an optimised-model claim.'),
   ('Pipeline slicing', 'm[:-1] creates the preparation-only view of the pipeline steps. Do not refit that view on held-out data; use it for transform or inspection.')],
  'The training flow is raw numeric measurements, median replacement, standardisation, then five-neighbour classification. Each step has one purpose. Imputation supplies the agreed partial-missing policy. Scaling makes distance comparisons use the training reference. The classifier stores the labelled training representation.\n\nFit the pipeline on the ninety training rows. You may evaluate the predeclared configuration on the thirty validation rows, inspect problems and decide whether to continue development. For this fixed demonstration we do not tune. If you choose to change the workflow, record that choice and use development evidence; do not consult the final test until the choice is frozen.\n\nThe example prints the configured neighbour count, imputation medians and scaler sample count. This is more informative than simply asserting that the word Pipeline appears in the source. The practice check compares the scaler mean against the mean of the imputed training table, which is the precise representation passed to it even if missingness is introduced in a later exercise.',
  'from lab import make_splits, build_model\nd = make_splits()\nm = build_model().fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nprint("Order:", list(m.named_steps))\nprint("k:", m.named_steps["classify"].n_neighbors)\nprint("Training medians:", m.named_steps["impute"].statistics_.round(3).tolist())\nprint("Scaler rows:", int(m.named_steps["scale"].n_samples_seen_))\nprint("Validation:", round(m.score(d["X_valid"], d["y_valid"]), 3))',
  'Compare the scaler mean with the mean of the actual imputed training representation.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom lab import make_splits, build_model\nd = make_splits()\nm = build_model().fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nfilled = m.named_steps["impute"].transform(d["X_train"])\nprint("Reference mean matches:", bool(np.allclose(m.named_steps["scale"].mean_, filled.mean(axis=0))))',
  'Show the number of neighbours and transformed input dimensions before final evaluation.',
  'from lab import make_splits, build_model\nd = make_splits()\nm = build_model().fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nprint("Neighbours:", m.named_steps["classify"].n_neighbors)\nprint("Prepared shape:", m[:-1].transform(d["X_train"]).shape)',
  [('Which rows fit the final lab pipeline?', 'The ninety training rows', 'All150 before splitting', 'The thirty test rows', 'The project deliberately keeps its training fit fixed for auditability.'),
   ('Does a frozen k=5 claim it is the optimal model?', 'No; it is a predeclared learning configuration', 'Yes', 'Only if validation is perfect', 'A fixed choice supports a clean demonstration, not an exhaustive comparison.')],
  'Refit the scaler on validation after deciding that the validation mean looks different.', 'Reuse the fitted training reference and investigate changed inputs without contaminating evaluation.',
  'The learned medians and scaler count come from the fitted objects. Pipeline order matters: the scaler is fitted on imputed training rows, not on a differently prepared table.')

L(5, '3. Report the final holdout with counts and a confusion matrix',
  'Evaluate the frozen pipeline and baseline on the same final rows and interpret errors.',
  [('Confusion matrix; diagonal; support', 'Rows count actual classes and columns count predicted classes in the stated label order. Diagonal cells are correct predictions; row totals are actual class support.'),
   ('run_experiment', 'The supplied final-report helper fits the fixed model and baseline on training, then reports train, validation and test separately. Running it inspects the final test.')],
  'Only after the configuration is frozen do we read the final holdout report. The helper fits the pipeline and majority baseline on training rows, then computes each partition’s counts and accuracy. It does not refit on validation or test. The final confusion matrix uses the fixed class order setosa, versicolor, virginica.\n\nRead one row at a time. Its sum is the number of evaluated flowers with that actual label. A diagonal entry is correct; an off-diagonal entry identifies which other label was predicted. This is more useful than celebrating a single rounded score. Thirty evaluation rows still provide limited evidence; one changed answer moves accuracy by about3.3 percentage points.\n\nThe test has now been inspected. Reproducing the same report verifies implementation, not a new independent model assessment. Do not use the visible errors to choose another k and present its result on the same thirty rows as an untouched test. More detailed precision, recall and threshold methods belong to Week46.',
  'from lab import run_experiment\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n_, _, report = run_experiment()\nfor part in ["train", "valid", "test"]:\n    s = report["scores"][part]\n    print(part, f"{s[\'correct\']}/{s[\'rows\']}", f"accuracy={s[\'accuracy\']:.3f}", f"baseline={s[\'baseline_accuracy\']:.3f}")\nmatrix = report["test_confusion_matrix"]\nprint("Actual rows; predicted columns:", matrix)\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6, 4.5))\nax.imshow(matrix, cmap="Blues", vmin=0)\nax.set(xticks=range(3), yticks=range(3), xticklabels=report["classes"], yticklabels=report["classes"], xlabel="Predicted class", ylabel="Actual class", title="Final Iris holdout: 30 flowers")\nfor i in range(3):\n    for j in range(3):\n        ax.text(j, i, str(matrix[i][j]), ha="center", va="center", color="white" if matrix[i][j] > 5 else "black")\nfig.tight_layout()\nfig.savefig("chart.png", dpi=110)\nplt.close(fig)',
  'Verify that the final matrix total is the test denominator and its diagonal is the correct count.',
  'from lab import run_experiment\n_, _, r = run_experiment()\nc = r["test_confusion_matrix"]\nprint("Matrix rows:", sum(map(sum, c)))\nprint("Correct diagonal:", sum(c[i][i] for i in range(3)))\nprint("Report correct:", r["scores"]["test"]["correct"])',
  'Calculate the final model’s percentage-point improvement over the specified baseline.',
  'from lab import run_experiment\n_, _, r = run_experiment()\ns = r["scores"]["test"]\nprint("Difference in percentage points:", round(100 * (s["accuracy"] - s["baseline_accuracy"]), 1))\nprint("Comparison is limited to this baseline and these rows")',
  [('What does a confusion-matrix row represent here?', 'One actual class across predicted classes', 'One model hyperparameter', 'One future dataset', 'Rows are actual labels and columns are predictions in the stated order.'),
   ('May the now-visible final errors guide a new supposedly untouched test result?', 'No', 'Yes if k changes only slightly', 'Only if performance improves', 'Using test feedback changes its role from independent final evidence.')],
  'Keep changing the model until the displayed final holdout matrix is perfect.', 'Report the frozen result and use fresh independent evaluation for subsequent changed claims.',
  'The bars are replaced here by integer cell counts so you can trace errors by class. The matrix total must equal30 and its diagonal sum must match the reported correct count.',
  figure=('Actual versus predicted Iris class counts on the final thirty-row holdout', 'Rows are actual labels; columns are predictions, in the printed class order. All cells sum to30. Off-diagonal values are observed mistakes, not probabilities.'))

L(5, '4. Inspect errors without rewriting the final evaluation',
  'Trace wrong predictions to row ids and distinguish diagnosis from a new test claim.',
  [('Error analysis', 'Inspect which cases fail, how labels and features were collected, and what limitation the pattern suggests. Diagnosis after a final test is useful, but later changes need fresh evaluation.'),
   ('Association; interpretation boundary', 'A neighbour model uses similarity in the transformed features. That behaviour is not proof that one feature causes the label or that a measurement change would change species.')],
  'Build a small table containing row id, actual label and predicted label. Keep only mismatches and map codes to readable names. This reveals what failed without changing the fitted pipeline. If a split has no errors, report that observed count; do not manufacture mistakes or infer perfect performance everywhere.\n\nInvestigate data and measurement context before blaming the algorithm. Similar measurements can occur across classes. A mistaken label, swapped unit or changed instrument can create different failure patterns. The four-column benchmark cannot answer every such question because it does not contain all provenance metadata.\n\nIt is reasonable to propose a next experiment from the errors. Clearly mark it as future development requiring new evaluation evidence. Do not delete awkward final rows, modify the target mapping or silently change the split to improve the report. Also avoid causal stories such as increasing a petal measurement turns one species into another: the model’s association is not a biological intervention.',
  'import pandas as pd\nfrom lab import run_experiment, LABELS\nm, d, _ = run_experiment()\nactual = d["y_test"]\npredicted = m.predict(d["X_test"])\nerrors = pd.DataFrame({"row_id": actual.index, "actual": [LABELS[int(n)] for n in actual], "predicted": [LABELS[int(n)] for n in predicted]})\nwrong = errors.loc[errors["actual"] != errors["predicted"]]\nprint("Errors:", len(wrong))\nprint(wrong.to_string(index=False))',
  'Compute per-class support and correct counts from the final confusion matrix.',
  'from lab import run_experiment\n_, _, r = run_experiment()\nfor i, name in enumerate(r["classes"]):\n    row = r["test_confusion_matrix"][i]\n    print(name, "correct", row[i], "of", sum(row))',
  'Write a next-step proposal that preserves the status of the inspected test.',
  'proposal = {"hypothesis": "changed measurement practice may hurt similarity", "next": "collect independently measured cases", "old_test": "already inspected; demonstration only"}\nfor key, value in proposal.items():\n    print(key + ": " + value)',
  [('Should final mistakes be deleted before publishing the score?', 'No; preserve the declared evaluation set', 'Yes if they are difficult', 'Only if there are few', 'Removing difficult cases changes the evaluation question and biases the result.'),
   ('Does a nearest-neighbour prediction explain a causal change in species?', 'No', 'Yes', 'Only if the nearest neighbour is close', 'Similarity-based prediction does not establish causal effects.')],
  'Remove the misclassified row and report an improved score without disclosure.', 'Keep the final counts and document hypotheses for a separately evaluated next experiment.',
  'The table preserves source row ids and pairs actual/predicted labels explicitly. Counting mismatches is diagnostic reporting; no model step is fitted or selected from these rows here.')

L(5, '5. Make a checked prediction for a new measurement row',
  'Apply the input contract and distinguish a predicted label from certainty.',
  [('Inference validation', 'Check the structure and known constraints before sending raw measurements through the fitted pipeline. Validation errors should be clear and should not trigger retraining.'),
   ('Out-of-scope input', 'A row can be numeric yet outside the model’s intended population. Unknown species, unrecorded measurement conventions and major range changes need additional evidence.')],
  'Use named centimetre columns rather than an anonymous four-item list when connecting the model to a caller. The helper restores the reviewed order and keeps the caller’s row identity. It returns a species name for the model’s predicted category, not a certificate of botanical identity.\n\nThe worked row is a manually supplied teaching measurement, not an independent labelled test case. Do not add its predicted name to the training dataset as if an expert verified it. The model always chooses among the recorded classes; it has no built-in guarantee that an unknown species will be rejected.\n\nTry invalid input deliberately. Missing a required column, supplying text or using an infinite value should fail clearly. A partially missing input uses the training imputation policy; an entirely missing row is rejected. Semantic errors such as positive millimetre values mislabeled as centimetres may still pass numeric checks, so a real interface must make units visible and validate its collection process.',
  'import pandas as pd\nfrom lab import make_splits, build_model, predict_checked, FEATURES\nd = make_splits()\nm = build_model().fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nnew = pd.DataFrame([[5.1, 3.5, 1.4, 0.2]], columns=FEATURES, index=["demo flower"])\nprint(predict_checked(m, new).to_dict())\nprint("Predicted category, not independently verified truth")',
  'Reject a new input missing one required feature column.',
  'from lab import make_splits, build_model, predict_checked\nd = make_splits()\nm = build_model().fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nrow = d["X_valid"].iloc[:1, :3]\ntry:\n    predict_checked(m, row)\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    print(str(error))',
  'Reject a completely unmeasured row instead of producing a plausible-looking answer.',
  'import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom lab import make_splits, build_model, predict_checked, FEATURES\nd = make_splits()\nm = build_model().fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\ntry:\n    predict_checked(m, pd.DataFrame([[np.nan] * 4], columns=FEATURES))\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    print(str(error))',
  [('Does a predicted name become a verified new training label?', 'No; obtain an independent label', 'Always', 'Only when printed as text', 'A model guess is not observed ground truth.'),
   ('Will this three-class model automatically recognise every unknown species?', 'No', 'Yes', 'Only with standardisation', 'Its output vocabulary is limited to the recorded training classes.')],
  'Treat any returned class name as certainty and append it as verified ground truth.', 'Label predictions as guesses within scope and obtain independent truth for evaluation or new labels.',
  'The named DataFrame records feature meanings and caller identity. predict_checked validates the contract and then uses the already fitted pipeline without learning from the new row.')

L(5, '6. Milestone: an honest offline Iris experiment',
  'Deliver reproducible results, inspectable predictions and a written explanation of limits.',
  [('Evaluation artifact', 'An inspectable output such as a JSON report and CSV of paired predictions, connecting scores to actual evaluated rows.'),
   ('Evidence versus repetition', 'Rerunning the same code and split supports reproducibility. New independent evaluation requires new relevant cases or a properly reserved evaluation design.')],
  'Finish the project by running python lab.py --output artifacts. Read the saved JSON report and prediction CSV rather than relying on a screenshot. The report records feature names, class order, model choice, seed, package versions, scores, confusion counts and limitations. The CSV gives one actual/predicted pair per final row. No executable model pickle is required for this foundations exercise.\n\nWrite a short conclusion: what was predicted, which inputs were used, how fitting and evaluation were separated, how the model compared with the majority baseline, where errors occurred and what evidence is still missing. Include a paragraph explaining underfitting, overfitting and the bias-variance intuition in your own words. Do not confuse those explanations with a formal variance estimate from one split.\n\nCompletion means you can reproduce and defend the workflow, not merely run it once. Rebuild the split and pipeline without copying, explain every new API, and solve the practice prompts before marking each part complete. The existing Previous/Next, notes, quizzes and progress controls remain your checkpoints. Use more study sessions if needed; understanding has priority over finishing a calendar day.',
  'import json\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom lab import write_report\nreport = write_report("artifacts")\nsaved = json.loads(Path("artifacts/report.json").read_text(encoding="utf-8"))\nrows = pd.read_csv("artifacts/test-predictions.csv")\nprint("Saved score matches:", saved["scores"] == report["scores"])\nprint("Prediction rows:", len(rows))\nprint("Recomputed accuracy:", round(float((rows.actual == rows.predicted).mean()), 3))\nprint("Limitations:", len(saved["limitations"]))',
  'Export twice and verify the same setup reproduces the same report rather than creating new independent evidence.',
  'from lab import write_report\na = write_report("first")\nb = write_report("second")\nprint("Same report:", a == b)\nprint("Same data and split, not new independent evidence")',
  'Create a completion checklist including an honest limitation and a written bias-variance explanation.',
  'required = ["task and units", "data provenance", "disjoint splits", "training-only preparation", "baseline comparison", "error counts", "bias-variance explanation", "limitations and next evidence"]\nfor number, item in enumerate(required, 1):\n    print(number, item)',
  [('What should accompany a final model score?', 'Task, split, baseline, counts, method and limitations', 'Only a claim that ML works', 'Only the best seed', 'A score needs context and traceable evidence to be understood.'),
   ('What does reproducing the same report demonstrate?', 'Repeatability of this setup', 'Independent new-population validation', 'Universal correctness', 'Repetition and independent evaluation answer different questions.')],
  'Submit only the highest accuracy number from repeated hidden experiments.', 'Submit the declared workflow, traceable artifacts and honest interpretation of its limits.',
  'The exported prediction pairs reproduce the JSON accuracy using ordinary pandas. This links the headline score to its actual thirty evaluated cases and makes the result reviewable.',
  shell='python lab.py --output artifacts\npython -m pytest -q\npython -m ruff check .\npython -m ruff format --check .')
